# Evidence for PF-CORPUS-PIN-001 (directory-scan survey + the fix)
# Survey only: no src/ change, no matrix flip, no ledger entry, no scenario, no
# runtime claim, no network, no GameClient launch, no canonical database access.
#
# WHAT THE SURVEY FOUND
# Thirteen places decide which files they are looking at by scanning a directory.
# Two of them (grade A) are in the same verifier, and the numbers they produce are
# pinned key-by-key in a published report and its regression test:
#   tools/pf_login_vital_req_static.py:941  **/LOGIN_*.txt   -> 63 files
#   tools/pf_login_vital_req_static.py:981  capture_v141/GAME_*.txt -> 69 files,
#                                           two of which (GAME_LIVE.txt,
#                                           GAME_EVENTS_LIVE.txt) the server
#                                           truncates and rewrites on every run.
# The frozen snapshot writes into BOTH scanned sets from a relative path
# (current/pf_login_game_server_v141.py:7867 capdir = Path("capture_v141");
#  :7401 GAME_{stamp}.txt; :7945 LOGIN_{stamp}.txt; :7372-7373 the live tails),
# so any boot with cwd = repository root moves both numbers, and capture_v141/ is
# git-ignored (.gitignore:157) so git cannot see it happen.  That is exactly the
# incident of 2026-08-19 (corpus 69 -> 72), which was found by accident.
#
# THE FIX THIS SURVEY PAID FOR
# docs/PF_CAPTURE_CORPUS.json names every archived capture with size and sha256;
# tools/pf_capture_corpus.py proves presence, byte-identity and - the part that
# would have caught the incident - that nothing unpinned is sitting in the
# directory; tests/test_capture_corpus.py enforces all three and proves the trap
# fires, on a throwaway tree.
#
# --- THE SURVEY ---
